const e="2026-11-05",t=[{title:"A Fiery Farewell: NASA's Swift Observatory Falls to Earth",source:"CNN, 4 November 2026 (NASA Swift Observatory reentry)",passage:`After nearly twenty-two years in orbit, NASA's Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory met a fiery end on Wednesday morning, plunging back into the Earth's atmosphere over Indonesia at around 7:39 a.m. Eastern Time. Data from the US Space Force placed the reentry of the 3,200-pound spacecraft above the archipelago, although it was not immediately clear whether any surviving debris reached land. For astronomers, the moment marked the close of an unusually versatile mission; for engineers, it raised fresh questions about how ageing satellites should be brought safely out of orbit.

Swift was launched from Cape Canaveral in Florida on 20 November 2004, atop a Delta II rocket, with a single, ambitious purpose: to study gamma-ray bursts, the most powerful explosions known in the universe. These brief, intense flashes, produced by collapsing stars and colliding neutron stars, can outshine entire galaxies for a few seconds. Yet Swift proved to be far more than a specialist instrument. Over two decades, scientists also used it to monitor other cosmic flashes, to track asteroids and comets, and to respond rapidly whenever a new transient event flared somewhere in the sky. A team at Penn State University, NASA's partner and home to the observatory's Mission Operations Center, sent the final kill-switch command on 20 October, deliberately shutting the spacecraft down before its orbit decayed beyond control.

Part of Swift's success lay in its speed. When its wide-field Burst Alert Telescope detected a gamma-ray burst, the spacecraft could slew, or turn, within minutes so that its narrower X-ray and ultraviolet telescopes could observe the fading afterglow while it was still bright. That rapid response allowed astronomers to pinpoint bursts with unusual precision, to measure their distances, and to identify the galaxies in which they occurred. Over time, the resulting catalogue transformed gamma-ray bursts from mysterious flashes into tools for studying the deaths of massive stars, the behaviour of matter under extreme conditions, and the expansion history of the early universe itself, a legacy far broader than its designers first imagined.

The observatory's demise was hastened by the Sun itself. As the Sun approached the peak of its eleven-year cycle in 2024, intense flares and coronal mass ejections caused the Earth's upper atmosphere to expand. That expansion increased the drag on Swift, pulling it gradually lower until it dropped to just 171 miles, about 275 kilometres, above the surface, and finally fell. It is a reminder that even the emptiness of low-Earth orbit is not truly empty: the thin gas at those altitudes exerts a real, cumulative force, and during periods of high solar activity that force grows substantially.

If Swift were launched today, its designers would face far stricter rules about its final chapter. Current orbital debris mitigation standards, updated in 2019, require that uncontrolled reentries carry an expected casualty risk of less than one in ten thousand, according to The Aerospace Corporation, a federally funded research and development centre. Swift, designed in an earlier era, had no established observational replacement waiting in the wings, and no controlled deorbit plan of the kind now expected for large observatories. Its uncontrolled return therefore belongs to a category that regulators are determined to shrink.

The same concern now shadows an even more famous observatory. NASA has recently asked commercial companies how they might boost, service or prolong the life of the Hubble Space Telescope, after analysis suggested a ten per cent chance that Hubble will plunge out of orbit by 2032 and a fifty per cent chance that it will deorbit by 2033. Swift's farewell thus serves as both an epitaph for a productive mission and a warning: the satellites we celebrate for what they reveal must also be planned, from the outset, for the day they come home.`,notes:[{word:"observatory",pos:"n.",meaning:"天文台；观测站"},{word:"reentry",pos:"n.",meaning:"（航天器）重返大气层"},{word:"debris",pos:"n.",meaning:"碎片；残骸"},{word:"gamma-ray burst",pos:"n.",meaning:"伽马射线暴"},{word:"transient",pos:"adj.",meaning:"短暂的；瞬变的"},{word:"coronal mass ejection",pos:"n.",meaning:"日冕物质抛射"},{word:"drag",pos:"n.",meaning:"阻力；拖曳"},{word:"decay",pos:"v.",meaning:"衰减；衰变"},{word:"mitigation",pos:"n.",meaning:"缓解；减轻"},{word:"casualty",pos:"n.",meaning:"伤亡；伤亡人数"},{word:"archipelago",pos:"n.",meaning:"群岛"},{word:"versatile",pos:"adj.",meaning:"多功能的；多才多艺的"},{word:"epitaph",pos:"n.",meaning:"墓志铭；挽辞"},{word:"prolong",pos:"v.",meaning:"延长；拖长"},{word:"cumulative",pos:"adj.",meaning:"累积的；渐增的"}],questions:[{q:"What was Swift's original purpose?",options:["To track asteroids only","To study gamma-ray bursts, the most powerful explosions in the universe","To replace the Hubble Space Telescope","To monitor weather on Earth"],answer:1},{q:"Why did Swift's orbit decay more quickly in recent years?",options:["Its fuel ran out in 2024","Increased solar activity expanded the upper atmosphere and increased drag","Penn State sent a command to push it downward","It collided with another satellite"],answer:1},{q:"What do current US debris mitigation standards require for uncontrolled reentries?",options:["That no debris reaches the ground at all","An expected casualty risk of less than one in 10,000","A controlled landing in the ocean","That the satellite burn up completely"],answer:1},{q:"What did the Penn State team do on 20 October?",options:["Launched a replacement observatory","Sent the final kill-switch command to shut Swift down","Announced a new gamma-ray burst","Moved Swift to a higher orbit"],answer:1},{q:"Surviving debris from Swift was confirmed to have landed on inhabited land.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:2},{q:"NASA estimates a 50 per cent chance that Hubble will deorbit by 2033.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"Swift was launched in 2004 and operated for nearly 22 years.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0}]},{title:"Fireballs in the November Sky: The Southern Taurid Meteor Shower",source:"CNN, 4 November 2026 (Southern Taurid meteor shower)",passage:`If you look up on a clear night this week, you may be rewarded with one of autumn's most spectacular sights: a fireball streaking slowly across the sky, its glowing trail lingering far longer than an ordinary shooting star. The Southern Taurid meteor shower, which began in September and runs until 20 November, is expected to be at its best at around midnight on Thursday, with its estimated peak at about 3 p.m. Eastern Time on 5 November, according to EarthSky. What makes the Taurids unusual is not their number, but their character.

Southern Taurid meteors strike the Earth's atmosphere at about 65,000 miles per hour, roughly 104,607 kilometres per hour. That sounds fast, yet by meteor standards it is comparatively slow, and the slower entry means each meteor remains visible for longer, producing particularly lengthy glowing trails. The meteors appear to radiate from the constellation of Taurus, the Bull, which gives the shower its name. On average, observers can expect only about five meteors per hour, a modest rate that would disappoint anyone chasing sheer quantity. The compensation lies in quality: the Taurids are, in the words of Bill Cooke, head of NASA's Meteoroid Environment Office, rich in bright fireballs, meteors bright enough to outshine the planets and occasionally to cast faint shadows.

The source of this display is comet 2P/Encke, whose debris trail the Earth passes through each autumn. Encke has the shortest orbital period of any known comet in the solar system, about 3.3 years, and over centuries it has shed a broad, diffuse stream of dust and pebbles along its path. When the Earth encounters the denser pockets within that stream, fireball activity increases, which is why some years produce a more memorable show than others. The Southern Taurids overlap with a second, nearby shower, the Northern Taurids, which began in mid-October, peaks on 12 November and ends on 10 December, so patient watchers may enjoy bright meteors for several weeks.

The distinction between a meteor, a meteoroid and a meteorite is worth keeping in mind while watching. A meteoroid is the solid particle travelling through space; a meteor is the streak of light produced when that particle burns up in the atmosphere; and a meteorite is the rare fragment that survives the journey and reaches the ground. Most Taurid particles are far too small to become meteorites, which is precisely why they are safe, and beautiful, to watch. In some years, however, the shower produces a marked increase in fireballs, when the Earth crosses a denser swarm within Encke's stream, and astronomers study those outbursts closely because they reveal the structure of a debris trail that cannot be seen directly.

For Cooke, however, meteor showers are not merely a spectacle. His work focuses on forecasting showers in order to protect crews and spacecraft from collisions with debris, a risk that grows as more satellites and stations occupy low-Earth orbit. A pea-sized particle travelling at tens of thousands of miles per hour carries enough energy to damage a solar panel or puncture a pressurised module, and advance warning allows operators to orient spacecraft or postpone spacewalks. The same physics that makes a fireball beautiful, in other words, makes it a hazard at orbital altitudes.

For most of us, the advice is simpler: find a dark spot away from street lights, let your eyes adapt for at least twenty minutes, and look up without concentrating on any single point in the sky. The Leonids follow on 16-17 November, but the Taurids offer something rarer than a crowded shower: the chance, on a quiet November night, to watch a single, slow fireball cross the darkness and feel, for a moment, the scale of the solar system passing overhead.`,notes:[{word:"meteor shower",pos:"n.",meaning:"流星雨"},{word:"fireball",pos:"n.",meaning:"火流星；大火球"},{word:"radiate",pos:"v.",meaning:"从一点向外发散；辐射"},{word:"constellation",pos:"n.",meaning:"星座"},{word:"orbital period",pos:"n.",meaning:"轨道周期"},{word:"debris",pos:"n.",meaning:"碎片；残骸"},{word:"diffuse",pos:"adj.",meaning:"扩散的；弥散的"},{word:"spectacle",pos:"n.",meaning:"壮观景象；奇观"},{word:"forecast",pos:"v.",meaning:"预测；预报"},{word:"puncture",pos:"v.",meaning:"刺穿；戳破"},{word:"pressurised",pos:"adj.",meaning:"加压的；增压的"},{word:"adapt",pos:"v.",meaning:"适应"},{word:"lingering",pos:"adj.",meaning:"持续的； lingering 的；久久不去的"},{word:"compensation",pos:"n.",meaning:"补偿；弥补"},{word:"modest",pos:"adj.",meaning:"不多的；适度的"}],questions:[{q:"Why are Southern Taurid meteors visible for longer than those of some other showers?",options:["They are larger than other meteors","They enter the atmosphere more slowly, so their glowing trails last longer","They fall closer to the Earth","They are made of a different material"],answer:1},{q:"What is the approximate hourly rate of the Southern Taurids?",options:["About five meteors per hour","About fifty meteors per hour","About five hundred meteors per hour","About one meteor per hour"],answer:0},{q:"What is the source of the Southern Taurid meteors?",options:["An asteroid belt near Mars","Debris left by comet 2P/Encke","Fragments of a destroyed satellite","Dust from the Moon"],answer:1},{q:"Why does NASA forecast meteor showers, according to the passage?",options:["To schedule telescope observations","To protect crews and spacecraft from debris collisions","To predict the weather","To choose launch dates for rockets"],answer:1},{q:"The Southern Taurid shower ends on 20 November.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"The Northern Taurids peak before the Southern Taurids.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"Fireballs from the Taurids are bright enough to be seen in daylight.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:2}]},{title:"Warming Seas Are Rewiring the Channel's Food Web",source:"Oceanographic Magazine, 4 November 2026 (Plymouth Marine Laboratory study)",passage:`Thirty-five years of patient observation off the coast of Plymouth have revealed a quiet transformation at the very base of the marine food web, one that could ripple upward to fish, seabirds and marine mammals. A new study, published on 4 November, combined decades of traditional zooplankton microscope records with eleven years of environmental DNA, or eDNA, data from seawater samples collected about six nautical miles south of Plymouth, in the English Channel. Its conclusion is unsettling: as the seas warm and their chemistry changes, smaller plankton are thriving while larger species decline, rewiring an ecosystem on which much larger life depends.

Plankton may be tiny, but their influence is planetary. Phytoplankton, the plant-like organisms that drift near the surface, produce more than fifty per cent of the Earth's oxygen, while also absorbing and locking away carbon and helping to regulate the global climate. Zooplankton, the tiny animals that eat them, include krill, copepods and arrow worms, and they form the crucial link between this microscopic plant life and everything above it, from small fish to whales. Change the composition of the plankton community, the researchers warn, and the consequences travel up the food chain in ways that are difficult to predict and harder still to reverse.

The study, led by Dr Karen Tait of Plymouth Marine Laboratory, found significant shifts in the chemical composition of the ocean over the study period, changes that influenced which plankton could flourish. Survival, it appears, depended increasingly on how organisms feed in response to declining nutrient availability: species able to exploit scarcer resources gained an advantage, while larger species that require richer food supplies lost ground. The combination of long microscope records and newer eDNA techniques, which detect traces of DNA shed into the water, allowed the team to see both who was present and how the community had changed over time, a picture neither method could have produced alone.

Warming itself helps to explain why nutrients become scarcer. As surface waters grow warmer, they become lighter and less likely to mix with the colder, nutrient-rich water below, a process oceanographers call stratification. In a strongly stratified sea, the surface layer where phytoplankton grow can become isolated from the deep reservoir of nutrients on which they depend, favouring small organisms that need less food and can survive on thinner supplies. That physical change, repeated across seasons and amplified over decades, is one reason why a gradual rise in temperature can produce a disproportionate ecological shift, even in a place where no single year looks dramatically different from the last.

The implications extend well beyond Plymouth. A shift towards smaller plankton could reduce the quality of food available to marine life, affecting fish size and populations, changing fisheries productivity, and even altering how much carbon sinks into the deep ocean, where it can be stored for centuries. Smaller, less nutritious prey may mean that seabirds must work harder to feed their chicks, and that commercially important fish grow more slowly, with knock-on effects for the coastal communities that depend on them. The Channel, in this sense, is not an isolated case but a window onto a broader pattern in warming seas worldwide.

None of this means that the ocean is emptying, and the researchers are careful not to overstate a single study. What the Plymouth record offers is rarer and more valuable: a long, continuous baseline against which change can be measured, and a warning that the most consequential shifts in an ecosystem may begin invisibly, among organisms too small to see, long before the larger animals that depend on them begin to disappear.`,notes:[{word:"zooplankton",pos:"n.",meaning:"浮游动物"},{word:"phytoplankton",pos:"n.",meaning:"浮游植物"},{word:"copepod",pos:"n.",meaning:"桡足类动物"},{word:"eDNA",pos:"n.",meaning:"环境 DNA"},{word:"nutrient",pos:"n.",meaning:"营养物质"},{word:"flourish",pos:"v.",meaning:"繁荣；茁壮成长"},{word:"composition",pos:"n.",meaning:"构成；成分"},{word:"ripple",pos:"v.",meaning:"（影响）扩散；波及"},{word:"knock-on effect",pos:"n.",meaning:"连锁反应；间接影响"},{word:"baseline",pos:"n.",meaning:"基线；基准"},{word:"fisheries",pos:"n.",meaning:"渔业"},{word:"seabird",pos:"n.",meaning:"海鸟"},{word:"microscopic",pos:"adj.",meaning:"微小的；显微镜下的"},{word:"exploit",pos:"v.",meaning:"利用；开发"},{word:"unsettling",pos:"adj.",meaning:"令人不安的"}],questions:[{q:"What did the Plymouth study combine?",options:["Satellite images and fishing records","35 years of zooplankton microscope records and 11 years of eDNA data","Interviews with fishermen and weather data","Two years of laboratory experiments"],answer:1},{q:"According to the passage, what share of the Earth's oxygen do phytoplankton produce?",options:["Less than 10 per cent","About 25 per cent","More than 50 per cent","Almost all of it"],answer:2},{q:"Which plankton are thriving as the seas warm, according to the study?",options:["Larger species","Smaller plankton","Only krill","Only arrow worms"],answer:1},{q:"What is one possible consequence of the shift towards smaller plankton?",options:["Fish grow faster","More carbon is stored permanently","Fish size and populations may be affected and fisheries productivity may change","Seabirds need less food"],answer:2},{q:"The seawater samples were collected about six nautical miles south of Plymouth.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"The study proves that all fish stocks in the Channel have already collapsed.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"The researchers claim that the ocean is emptying.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1}]}],a={date:e,newsReadings:t};export{e as date,a as default,t as newsReadings};
